{
  "dom_id": "step-q338954",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14250470/assets/8c0a344d98dc0ca3a6e4c0fdc3c98074bc2de23301300e663a02ea6f4dff3035.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Therefore\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,6}f(x)=$\n\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "We see from the graph that as $x$ approaches $6$ from the left, the function $f(x)$ approaches $\\infty .$ So, the left-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{-}}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nLikewise, as $x$ approaches $6$ from the right, the function $f(x)$ again approaches $\\infty .$ So, the right-hand limit is\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {6}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 6}f(x)=\\infty \\,.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
